var BANK_PARTS = BANK_PARTS || [];
BANK_PARTS.push([
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":2,"s":"Offices that switched to standing desks reported fewer back complaints the next month than offices that kept seated desks. The safety lead concludes that the standing desks caused the reduction.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["Before the switch, the two groups had similar back-complaint rates and similar hours, and neither group changed its chairs or its workload.","Employees who already had fewer back complaints were more likely to move to the offices that switched to standing desks.","Standing desks cost more than seated desks.","The safety lead writes a monthly summary.","Seated desks have been sold under the same brand for years."],"a":0,"e":"Matching the groups beforehand and holding chairs and workload fixed removes the obvious alternatives. The desk change is then better support for the causal claim. The attractive choice — Employees who already had fewer back complaints were more likely to move to the offices that switched to standing desks. — loses because it uses the right groups and the wrong direction. If the switchers were already the low-complaint employees, the gap belongs to a prior difference and the causal claim is weaker."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":3,"s":"Most people who used a park kiosk on one afternoon favored closing the lower lawn to picnics. The ranger concludes that most of the park's visitors favor the closure.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The kiosk screen stays readable in direct sun.","On the picnic question, kiosk users give the same answers, at the same rates, as visitors who never touch the kiosk.","The lower lawn is mowed on Thursdays.","The ranger trained in another county.","Picnic tables are made of cedar."],"a":1,"e":"The gap is whether kiosk users speak for visitors who walk past. Matching answers close that gap. The attractive choice — The kiosk screen stays readable in direct sun. — loses because a readable screen might explain how a vote gets cast outdoors. It does not show that the people who stop match the people who never do."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":2,"s":"Glazed relief tiles were 47 percent of a showroom's line items in the spring quarter and 12 percent in the fall quarter. The buyer concludes that the showroom moved fewer glazed relief tiles in the fall.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["A relief tile takes longer to crate than the flat rugs displayed beside it.","The buyer once apprenticed with a muralist.","The showroom recorded fewer line items in the fall quarter than in the spring quarter.","Relief tiles are crated in straw.","The showroom replaced its street awning before the fall."],"a":2,"e":"The share fell and the number of line items also fell. A smaller slice of a smaller set of line items is fewer relief tiles, so the buyer's count is supported. The attractive choice — A relief tile takes longer to crate than the flat rugs displayed beside it. — loses because crating time may explain why a customer chooses a rug. It does not compare the spring and fall totals, so the share-to-count step stays open."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Weak analogy","d":3,"s":"A mesh cover reduced bird losses on a blueberry row. The grower concludes that the same mesh will reduce bird losses on a fig tree.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The fig tree is older than the blueberry plants.","Figs ripen later than blueberries.","The grower prefers one brand of mesh.","Birds take the figs in the same way they took the blueberries, and the mesh is what stopped them.","Both plantings are inside the same fence."],"a":3,"e":"The transfer needs the same kind of loss. Matching the way the fruit is taken, and identifying the mesh as what stopped it, supplies that likeness. The attractive choice — The fig tree is older than the blueberry plants. — loses because age is a difference that need not matter to birds. An older tree can still lose fruit the same way. The attractive comparison does not show the mechanism matches."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":2,"s":"The inspector who wrote the city's kitchen-hood code applied the code's clearance rule to this restaurant's measurements and concluded that the hood should be raised. The owner concludes that the hood should be raised.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The inspector also paints watercolor pictures of restaurant kitchens.","A previous hood on this street was made of copper.","The owner meets suppliers on Tuesdays.","The kitchen faces an alley.","The measurements were taken at the heights the code specifies, and the clearance rule is the standard the city uses for this kind of hood."],"a":4,"e":"The conclusion rests on the code's application. Confirming the method and the governing rule tightens that support. The attractive choice — The inspector also paints watercolor pictures of restaurant kitchens. — loses because watercolor skill is a kind of attention that is not the code. The argument is stronger because of the rule and the measurements, not because the inspector paints."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"False dichotomy","d":3,"s":"Either the organ is retuned to the new pitch or the chorus will sing out of tune. The organ will not be retuned. Therefore, the chorus will sing out of tune.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The chorus must sing at the new pitch, and retuning the organ is the only way that chorus can match it.","The organ was built before 1900.","The chorus rehearses on Thursdays.","Programs list the organist's name.","The new pitch is written on the score."],"a":0,"e":"The conclusion needs the refusal to retune to leave the chorus out of tune. A requirement to sing at that pitch, plus no other way to match it, locks that result in. The attractive choice — The organ was built before 1900. — loses because the year of construction does not show that the chorus lacks another way to match the pitch. An old organ can still be irrelevant to whether the singers can adjust."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":2,"s":"No line in the foundry's heat book mentions a cold pour. The founder concludes that there was no cold pour during the weeks the book covers.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The founder has held the job longer than anyone else now in the shop.","Any cold pour in those weeks would have been entered before the founder read the book, and no page from those weeks is missing.","The molds are numbered in chalk.","Metal is delivered on Monday.","The heat book has a cloth cover."],"a":1,"e":"Silence supports \"no cold pour\" once a cold pour would have been entered and the pages are all there. The attractive choice — The founder has held the job longer than anyone else now in the shop. — loses because years on the job do not show that cold pours are recorded. A long-serving founder can still be reading an incomplete book."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Circular reasoning","d":3,"s":"Barometer A is called accurate because it matches barometer B, and B is called accurate because it matches A. A clerk concludes that barometer A is accurate.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["Barometer A was manufactured more recently than barometer B.","Both barometers hang on the same wall.","A mercury standard, set from neither barometer, matches barometer A.","The clerk reads them in the morning.","Mercury is denser than water."],"a":2,"e":"The clerk's conclusion is that A is accurate. An independent standard that matches A supports that accuracy without traveling through the circle. The attractive choice — Barometer A was manufactured more recently than barometer B. — loses because a newer manufacture does not show that A agrees with a real pressure. Age leaves the circle where it was."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":3,"s":"The theater's fly rope has held the Sunday drop for six years. The stage manager concludes that it will hold this Sunday's drop.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["This Sunday's drop is the heaviest the theater has ever hung on that rope.","The Sunday drop is a painted scenic border.","The crew watches from the wings.","This Sunday's drop is no heavier than the lightest drop the rope has held, and an inspection yesterday found the rope unchanged.","The manager learned the six-year record from one card."],"a":3,"e":"A past success supports the next drop when the new load stays within the loads already held and the rope has not changed. The attractive choice — This Sunday's drop is the heaviest the theater has ever hung on that rope. — loses because a heavier drop than any past success is a reason the record does not reach this Sunday. That cuts against the conclusion."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Part-to-whole","d":2,"s":"Each tile in the mosaic stack weighs less than one clerk can lift. The builder concludes that the stack weighs less than one clerk can lift.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["Each tile weighs less than the stack weighs.","The tiles are glazed on one side.","The builder prefers a wooden pallet.","Most stacks in the shop contain square tiles.","The stack weighs no more than its heaviest tile."],"a":4,"e":"If the stack is no heavier than its heaviest tile, and every tile is liftable, the stack is liftable. The attractive choice — Each tile weighs less than the stack weighs. — loses because each tile being lighter than the whole is ordinary and points the wrong way. It does not cap the stack at one clerk's limit."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":2,"s":"Schools that added a second recess reported fewer afternoon nurse visits the next month than schools that did not. The nurse concludes that the second recess caused the reduction.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["Before the change, the two groups of schools had similar visit rates and similar schedules, and neither group changed its class length.","Schools that already had fewer nurse visits were more likely to add the second recess.","A second recess costs staff time.","The nurse files a report each term.","Playgrounds are inspected in the spring."],"a":0,"e":"A matched start and a stable class length leave the recess as the remaining difference. That supports the causal claim. The attractive choice — Schools that already had fewer nurse visits were more likely to add the second recess. — loses because if the schools that added recess were already the low-visit schools, the gap is a prior difference. That weakens the claim."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":2,"s":"Travel essays fell from 40 percent of a journal's pages to 10 percent. The editor concludes that the journal printed fewer travel-essay pages in the later issue.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["Travel essays take longer to fact-check than the journal's other pages.","The later issue contained fewer pages than the earlier issue.","The editor once lived abroad.","Travel essays are set in a serif face.","The journal's cover was redesigned."],"a":1,"e":"A smaller share of a smaller issue is fewer travel pages. The editor's count is supported. The attractive choice — Travel essays take longer to fact-check than the journal's other pages. — loses because fact-checking time does not compare the two issues' lengths. The share-to-count step stays open."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Weak analogy","d":2,"s":"A lid reduced spills from open paint cans that were lost when the can was jogged. The shop concludes that a lid will reduce spills from an open acid bath.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The paint shop is older than the room that holds the acid bath.","Paint is sold by the gallon.","The acid bath is spilled by the same jogging of an open top, and a lid is what stopped that jogging loss in the paint cans.","The shop opens at eight.","Acid is stored in glass."],"a":2,"e":"The conclusion needs the acid to be lost the way the paint was. Matching that jog, and the lid's role in stopping it, supports the transfer. The attractive choice — The paint shop is older than the room that holds the acid bath. — loses because the age of the paint shop does not show how the acid is spilled. An older shop can teach nothing about a fume or a jog."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":3,"s":"The inspector who wrote the city's elevator bulletin applied its wear rule to this cab's readings and recommended replacement. The board concludes that the cab should be replaced.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The inspector collects model trains.","The cab serves twelve floors.","The board meets on the first Monday.","The readings were taken by the method the bulletin specifies, and the wear rule is the standard the city uses for this replacement.","The bulletin is printed on gray paper."],"a":3,"e":"The board is relying on the bulletin. Confirming the method and the rule makes that reliance more secure. The attractive choice — The inspector collects model trains. — loses because a model-train hobby is not the wear rule. It does not tighten the link between the readings and the replacement."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":2,"s":"No line in the creamery's vat book mentions a sour batch. The cheesemaker concludes that no batch soured during the weeks the book covers.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The cheesemaker has made cheese longer than anyone else in the room.","The vats are numbered.","Milk is delivered at dawn.","The book has a linen cover.","Any sour batch in those weeks would have been entered before the book was read, and no page is missing."],"a":4,"e":"A complete book that would have recorded a sour batch, and does not, supports the conclusion that none soured. The attractive choice — The cheesemaker has made cheese longer than anyone else in the room. — loses because years at the vat do not show that sour batches are written down. Tenure leaves the recording gap open."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":2,"s":"The chapel roof has shed every recorded hailstorm for a decade. The sexton concludes that it will shed tomorrow's hail.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["Tomorrow's hail is forecast to be no larger than the smallest storm the roof has shed, and an inspection found the roof unchanged.","Tomorrow's hail is forecast to be larger than any storm the roof has faced.","The chapel roof is made of slate.","Parishioners stand under the porch.","The sexton read the decade from one diary."],"a":0,"e":"A decade of shedding supports tomorrow when the new hail stays within those storms and the roof has not changed. The attractive choice — Tomorrow's hail is forecast to be larger than any storm the roof has faced. — loses because a larger storm than any success is a reason the decade does not reach tomorrow. It works against the conclusion."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":2,"s":"Most people who spoke at a microphone during one rally favored closing the city pool. The organizer concludes that most residents favor the closure.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The microphone was loud enough to be heard at the back of the rally.","On the pool question, people who speak at that microphone match residents who never speak, at the same rates.","The city pool the rally discussed is outdoors.","The organizer lives nearby.","Rallies are held on Saturdays."],"a":1,"e":"The gap is the step from speakers to residents. A match on this question supports the organizer's generalization. The attractive choice — The microphone was loud enough to be heard at the back of the rally. — loses because volume explains whether a speech carried. It does not show that speakers think like people who stayed home."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"False dichotomy","d":2,"s":"Either the score is cut or the premiere misses the printer. The score will not be cut. Therefore, the premiere will miss the printer.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The printer has enough paper for an uncut score.","The score was copied on Wednesday.","No overtime, no second printer, and no other cut can save the date, and the score's length is what would make the printer miss it.","The premiere is on a Friday.","The printer has missed other dates."],"a":2,"e":"The conclusion needs the uncut score to miss the date. Ruling out every other save, and tying the miss to the length, supports that result. The attractive choice — The printer has enough paper for an uncut score. — loses because paper on hand would matter if the pages were ready and too bulky. It does not show that the uncut score can be finished on time."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Ad hominem","d":3,"s":"Because the chemist's spouse sells filters, a blogger dismisses the chemist's well test and concludes that the test does not warrant changing the filter.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The spouse sold more filters in the latest quarter than in the quarter before.","The well is behind the house.","Filters are stocked in three sizes.","The vials were switched, before the test was read, with vials from a different well that the chemist did not sample.","The blogger posts on Fridays."],"a":3,"e":"The blogger claims the test does not warrant a change. Switched vials, from a well the chemist did not sample, give a reason the test fails to speak to this well. The attractive choice — The spouse sold more filters in the latest quarter than in the quarter before. — loses because higher filter sales deepen the family interest the blogger already cites. They do not show that the test itself fails to warrant a change."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":2,"s":"Blocks that hung bat boxes had fewer mosquitoes the next month than blocks that did not. The ecologist concludes that the boxes caused the reduction.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["Blocks that already had fewer mosquitoes were more likely to hang the boxes.","Bat boxes are made of cedar.","The ecologist counts at dusk.","Mosquitoes are more active after rain.","Before the boxes, the blocks had similar mosquito counts, and neither group drained water or changed its lighting."],"a":4,"e":"A matched start, with water and lighting held fixed, leaves the boxes as the cited difference. That supports the ecologist. The attractive choice — Blocks that already had fewer mosquitoes were more likely to hang the boxes. — loses because if the boxed blocks were already the low-mosquito blocks, the gap is prior. That weakens rather than strengthens."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":3,"s":"Classrooms that added skylights reported fewer afternoon headaches the next month than classrooms that did not. The principal concludes that the skylights caused the reduction.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["In that month, only the classrooms that added skylights also replaced their fluorescent tubes, and headache rates had matched before the month began.","Headache logs are a poor way to rank the architectural quality of a classroom.","Skylights cost more than tubes.","The principal's memo is dated on a Monday.","Fluorescent tubes are sold in boxes of ten."],"a":0,"e":"The new tubes are a difference present only in the rooms that improved, and the rooms had matched before. That is an alternative cause for the headache gap. The attractive choice — Headache logs are a poor way to rank the architectural quality of a classroom. — loses because the conclusion is about headaches, not about architectural quality. A log can be a bad quality index and still record a real drop in headaches."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":2,"s":"Most diners who texted a number printed on the receipt favored closing the patio. The owner concludes that most diners favor the closure.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["Several texters said they would still come for dinner if the patio stayed open.","Diners who text that number are far more likely than other diners to want the patio closed, and most diners never text.","The receipt is printed on thermal paper.","The patio faces the alley.","Dinner service starts at five."],"a":1,"e":"If texters are systematically more anti-patio than diners who do not text, the text majority misrepresents the room the owner describes. The attractive choice — Several texters said they would still come for dinner if the patio stayed open. — loses because willingness to come anyway is a different attitude from the vote to close the patio. It can be true of texters and still leave their votes typical or not."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":2,"s":"Commissioned portraits fell from half of a studio's sittings to a fifth. The bookkeeper concludes that the studio held fewer portrait sittings in the later season.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["A portrait sitting takes longer than a passport photo.","The bookkeeper studied accounting.","The studio booked four times as many sittings in the later season as in the earlier season.","Portraits are shot against a gray cloth.","The studio moved its chair."],"a":2,"e":"A one-fifth share of a total four times larger is more portrait sittings, not fewer. The bookkeeper's count is undermined. The attractive choice — A portrait sitting takes longer than a passport photo. — loses because duration may explain scheduling. It does not compare the two seasons' totals, so it does not show the count rose or fell."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Weak analogy","d":3,"s":"A gravel path reduced puddles on a flat yard. The builder concludes that gravel will reduce puddles on a steep roof.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["The roof is newer than the yard path.","Gravel is quarried nearby.","The builder starts work at seven.","The roof's puddles come from ice dams at the eaves, and gravel does not drain an ice dam.","The yard is fenced."],"a":3,"e":"The yard result was about standing water on a flat surface. Ice dams are a different mechanism, so the gravel transfer is undercut. The attractive choice — The roof is newer than the yard path. — loses because newness does not say how the roof holds water. A newer roof can puddle for the same reason or a different one. Age is not the damaging fact."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":2,"s":"A poet, citing no borings, says the town should move the seawall inland. The council concludes that the wall should be moved.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["The poet once published a mocking portrait of the council's chair.","The poet lives in another town.","The wall is inspected each spring.","The council publishes agendas on Monday.","The coastal engineer's borings, under the standard the council must apply, require the wall to stay on its present line."],"a":4,"e":"The conclusion has no coastal evidence. Borings under the governing standard, pointing the other way, are direct evidence against the move. The attractive choice — The poet once published a mocking portrait of the council's chair. — loses because a mocking portrait is a personal grievance. It does not bear on where the wall should stand."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"False dichotomy","d":2,"s":"Either the chorus drops the encore or the concert misses the last train. The encore will not be dropped. Therefore, the concert will miss the last train.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["A later train, already on the schedule, has carried this chorus home after longer encores, and the chorus will use it.","The last train has enough seats for the chorus.","The encore was rehearsed on Tuesday.","Tickets mention the encore.","The chorus has missed trains before."],"a":0,"e":"The conclusion needs keeping the encore to miss the train. A later train that the chorus will use breaks that horn. The attractive choice — The last train has enough seats for the chorus. — loses because seats on the train they are said to miss do not show that another train can save them. Capacity on the threatened train leaves the dilemma standing."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":2,"s":"No line in the dovecote log mentions a lost bird. The keeper concludes that no bird was lost during the weeks the log covers.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["The keeper has tended the cote longer than any neighbor.","Lost birds are chalked on a slate in the loft, and the log is never copied from that slate.","The dovecote faces south toward the orchard.","Feed is measured in a tin.","The log has a paper cover."],"a":1,"e":"If losses are written somewhere the log does not copy, a silent log is compatible with lost birds. The conclusion is undercut. The attractive choice — The keeper has tended the cote longer than any neighbor. — loses because years with the birds do not show that the log is the record of losses. Tenure does not put a lost bird into a book that does not receive the slate."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Circular reasoning","d":3,"s":"Sundial A is called right because it matches sundial B, and B is called right because it matches A. A visitor concludes that sundial A is right.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["Sundial A was carved more recently than sundial B.","Both dials are in the same garden.","A radio time signal, set from neither dial, disagrees with sundial A.","The visitor reads them at noon.","The radio is kept indoors."],"a":2,"e":"The visitor's conclusion is that A is right. An independent signal that disagrees shows A is not right. The attractive choice — Sundial A was carved more recently than sundial B. — loses because a more recent carving does not show that A is wrong, or right. Age leaves the mutual match untouched."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":3,"s":"The scaffold has held the painter's usual plank at every job this year. The painter concludes that it will hold tomorrow's plank.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["The painter has bought planks from the same mill all year.","The painter prefers a wide brush.","Jobs are listed on a card.","Tomorrow's plank is far heavier than any plank the scaffold has held, and a joint opened after the last job.","The scaffold is stored indoors."],"a":3,"e":"The record is lighter planks on a joint that had not yet opened. A heavier plank plus a new opening undercuts the projection. The attractive choice — The painter has bought planks from the same mill all year. — loses because one mill for the whole year, if anything, keeps the past planks comparable. It does not show that tomorrow's load or the joint has changed."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Part-to-whole","d":2,"s":"Each glass pane weighs less than the suction cup is rated to lift. The glazier concludes that the crate of panes weighs less than that rating.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["The panes were manufactured in the same year.","The glass panes are clear rather than frosted.","The glazier wears gloves.","Suction cups are sold in pairs.","Even if every pane were only as heavy as the lightest pane in the crate, their combined weight would exceed the cup's rating."],"a":4,"e":"If the lightest pane, multiplied across the crate, already exceeds the rating, the crate is over the rating. The glazier's conclusion is false. The attractive choice — The panes were manufactured in the same year. — loses because a shared year of manufacture does not add the panes together. The crate can still be under the rating or over it either way."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":3,"s":"Offices that added a nap room reported fewer sick days the next quarter than offices that did not. The director concludes that the nap room caused the reduction.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["In that quarter, only the offices that added a nap room also cut the workweek, and sick-day rates had matched before the quarter.","Sick-day logs are a poor way to rank the culture of an office.","Nap rooms require a couch.","The director's memo is two pages.","Sick days are entered in a spreadsheet."],"a":0,"e":"The shorter week is present only where sick days fell, and the offices had matched before. That alternative undercuts the nap-room claim. The attractive choice — Sick-day logs are a poor way to rank the culture of an office. — loses because the conclusion is about sick days, not about culture. A log can be a bad culture index and still show a real drop in sick days."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":2,"s":"Reprint pages fell from 25 percent of a press's output to 5 percent. The scheduler concludes that the press ran fewer reprint pages in the later month.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["A reprint page costs more to run than a first-run page.","The press ran five times as many pages in the later month as in the earlier month.","The scheduler once ran a press.","Reprint pages are marked with a stamp.","The press room was painted."],"a":1,"e":"Five percent of a fivefold output equals 25 percent of the earlier output, so the later month did not run fewer reprint pages. The count conclusion fails. The attractive choice — A reprint page costs more to run than a first-run page. — loses because cost per page does not compare the two months' totals. Expense leaves the arithmetic where it was."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Weak analogy","d":2,"s":"A door bell reduced thefts from an open coat rack, where passersby were the problem. The manager concludes that a door bell will reduce thefts from locked staff lockers.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["The locker room was built more recently than the coat rack.","The door bells are made of brass.","Locker thefts are committed by people who already have keys, and a bell at the door does not announce them.","The manager arrives at eight.","The staff lockers are numbered in paint."],"a":2,"e":"The bell worked against passersby. Key-holders are a different problem, so the transfer is undercut. The attractive choice — The locker room was built more recently than the coat rack. — loses because the age of the room does not identify who takes the contents. A newer locker room can still be robbed by passersby or by key-holders."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":3,"s":"The author of the county dam manual says this dam should be lowered, and the board concludes that it should be lowered.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["The author collects postcards of dams.","The dam was built of local stone.","The board meets monthly.","Applied to this dam's own readings, the manual's table requires the dam to stay at its present height, and the author misread a digit.","The manual is a bound volume."],"a":3,"e":"The board is following the author. The manual's own table, correctly read, points the other way and undercuts that following. The attractive choice — The author collects postcards of dams. — loses because postcards are a hobby. They do not show that the table was misread or that the height should stay."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":2,"s":"No entry in the creamery's other notebook, the culture book, mentions a failed culture. The maker concludes that no culture failed during the weeks that book covers.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["The maker is the oldest person in the creamery.","Cultures are kept in glass.","The cooling room is downstairs.","The book is ruled in blue.","Failed cultures are written on a board in the cooling room, and the culture book is not copied from that board."],"a":4,"e":"A silent book that does not receive the real record is compatible with failed cultures. The conclusion is undercut. The attractive choice — The maker is the oldest person in the creamery. — loses because being the oldest maker does not move a failure from the board into the book. Tenure is not a recording rule."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":2,"s":"The slate roof has shed ten years of ordinary storms. The owner concludes that it will shed tomorrow's storm.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["Tomorrow's storm is far beyond any storm the roof has shed, and a crack opened in the slate after the last one.","The house has had this roof for the whole ten years.","The slate on this roof is gray.","The owner watches storms from the kitchen.","Ten is the number painted on the gate."],"a":0,"e":"The record is ordinary storms on a roof that had not yet cracked. A worse storm plus a new crack undercuts the owner. The attractive choice — The house has had this roof for the whole ten years. — loses because owning the roof for the whole record keeps the past cases on this roof. It does not show that tomorrow is ordinary or that the slate is intact."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":2,"s":"Most people who called a radio show on one evening favored moving the statue. The host concludes that most residents favor the move.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["Several callers said they admire the sculptor.","Callers that evening are far more likely than other residents to favor the move, and most residents never call the show.","The show lasts an hour.","The statue in the square is cast bronze.","The host works the evening shift."],"a":1,"e":"If callers are systematically more in favor than people who do not call, the evening majority does not represent residents. The attractive choice — Several callers said they admire the sculptor. — loses because admiration for the sculptor is a different attitude from the vote to move the statue. It does not show whether callers match everyone else."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"False dichotomy","d":2,"s":"Either the appendix is cut or the book misses the fair. The appendix will not be cut. Therefore, the book will miss the fair.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["The fair has enough table space for a book that includes the appendix.","The appendix was drafted in May.","A second binder, already scheduled, has delivered books with this appendix on tighter deadlines and is assigned to this book.","The fair is on a Saturday.","The publisher has cut appendices before."],"a":2,"e":"The conclusion needs the appendix to miss the fair. A second binder who can finish it on time breaks that horn. The attractive choice — The fair has enough table space for a book that includes the appendix. — loses because table space would matter if the book were ready and too bulky to display. It does not show that the appendix can be bound in time."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Ad hominem","d":3,"s":"Because the surveyor's cousin sells lot surveys, a commentator dismisses the surveyor's boundary reading and concludes that the reading does not warrant moving the fence.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["The cousin sold more lot surveys in the latest quarter than in the quarter before.","The lot is behind the survey office.","Fence posts come in three lengths.","A second surveyor with no survey business, using the same monuments, read the same boundary and recommended the same fence move.","The commentator publishes on Thursdays."],"a":3,"e":"The commentator's only ground is the cousin. An uninvolved surveyor reading the same monuments the same way shows the recommendation does not depend on that interest. The attractive choice — The cousin sold more lot surveys in the latest quarter than in the quarter before. — loses because higher sales deepen the family story the commentator already uses. They support setting the surveyor aside. They do not show that the reading fails to warrant a move."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":2,"s":"Ponds that received floating reed islands had fewer algae mats the next month than ponds that did not. A neighbor concludes that the islands caused the reduction.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["Algae counts are a poor way to rank how pleasant a pond is to walk past.","The reed islands are made of coir.","The neighbor counts at noon.","The ponds face the street.","Only the ponds that received islands also stopped taking lawn runoff, and the mats had matched the month before."],"a":4,"e":"The stopped runoff is a difference present only where the mats thinned, and the ponds had matched before. That undercuts the island explanation. The attractive choice — Algae counts are a poor way to rank how pleasant a pond is to walk past. — loses because the conclusion is about algae mats, not about pleasantness. A count can be a bad pleasure index and still be a real count."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":3,"s":"Most letters the planning office received opposed a skate park. The clerk concludes that most residents oppose the skate park.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["People who write the planning office are far more likely to oppose a skate park than residents who do not write, and most residents never write.","Several letter writers said they like the look of concrete bowls.","The office is downtown.","Letters are date-stamped.","The clerk files them in a drawer."],"a":0,"e":"If writers are systematically more opposed than residents who stay silent, the letters misrepresent the city the clerk describes. The attractive choice — Several letter writers said they like the look of concrete bowls. — loses because liking the look of a bowl is a different attitude from opposing the park. It does not test whether writers match everyone else."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":2,"s":"Sidebar essays fell from 30 percent of a magazine's pieces to 8 percent. The editor concludes that the magazine ran fewer sidebar essays in the later issue.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["A sidebar essay is paid at a lower rate than a feature.","The later issue contained four times as many pieces as the earlier issue.","The editor prefers short sentences.","Sidebars are set in a narrow measure.","The magazine changed its paper."],"a":1,"e":"Eight percent of a fourfold issue can be more sidebars than 30 percent of the old issue. The editor's count is undermined. The attractive choice — A sidebar essay is paid at a lower rate than a feature. — loses because the pay rate does not compare how many pieces the two issues contained. Pay leaves the share-to-count step open."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Conditional chain","d":3,"s":"If a medal is engraved, it is catalogued. If it is catalogued, a photograph is filed. Medal 7 has been engraved. Medal 8 has not been catalogued.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["A photograph has been filed for medal 8.","Medal 7 has not been catalogued.","A photograph has been filed for medal 7.","Medal 8 was engraved.","Every medal with a photograph on file has been engraved."],"a":2,"e":"Engraving brings a catalogue entry, and a catalogue entry brings a photograph. Medal 7 was engraved, so both steps apply to it. The attractive choice — A photograph has been filed for medal 8. — loses because medal 8 was not catalogued, so the second conditional never starts for it. Nothing files a photograph of medal 8. The numeral is easy to swap with 7."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":2,"s":"A bakery sold the same number of pies in June and in July. Fruit pies were a smaller share of the pies in July than in June.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["The bakery sold fewer pies of every kind in July than in June.","Fruit pies take longer to bake than nut pies.","The bakery will sell fewer fruit pies in August.","The bakery sold fewer fruit pies in July than in June.","No nut pie was sold in June."],"a":3,"e":"The total is unchanged and fruit's share fell, so the fruit count fell. The attractive choice — The bakery sold fewer pies of every kind in July than in June. — loses because only the fruit share is said to fall. With the total fixed, some other kind of pie took a larger share and may have grown."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Sufficient/necessary confusion","d":3,"s":"A kiln firing is authorized only if the clay has passed the moisture test. This clay has not passed the moisture test.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["This clay will be authorized for a kiln firing if it passes the moisture test.","Every clay that passes the moisture test is authorized for a firing.","This clay was mixed on a Monday.","Moisture tests cost more this year than last year.","This clay has not been authorized for a kiln firing."],"a":4,"e":"\"Only if\" makes the moisture test necessary for authorization. No pass, therefore no authorization. The attractive choice — This clay will be authorized for a kiln firing if it passes the moisture test. — loses because passing the test is not said to be enough. The rule can require the test and still withhold a firing for a second reason. The attractive sentence turns a necessary condition into a sufficient one."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Conditional reversal/negation","d":2,"s":"No one who skipped the safety talk may enter the casting floor. Idris entered the casting floor this morning. June skipped the safety talk.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["June may not enter the casting floor.","Idris attended every talk the plant offered this year.","June entered the casting floor this morning.","Idris skipped the safety talk.","The floor Idris entered had been swept before the talk."],"a":0,"e":"Skipping the talk bars entry. June skipped it, so June may not enter. The attractive choice — Idris attended every talk the plant offered this year. — loses because Idris entered, so Idris did not skip this talk. The statements say nothing about the other talks on the plant calendar."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Part-to-whole","d":2,"s":"Every crew that works the night shift is assigned its own locker, and no locker is shared. The plant has 30 lockers. Forty-five crews are on the roster.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["At least 15 of the rostered crews are unqualified for the night shift.","At most 30 of the rostered crews can work the night shift at the same time.","The plant will buy 15 more lockers.","Some lockers hold two crews when the roster is full.","Every rostered crew worked last night."],"a":1,"e":"Each crew on the shift uses a distinct locker, and there are 30 lockers. No arrangement puts more than 30 crews on that shift together. The attractive choice — At least 15 of the rostered crews are unqualified for the night shift. — loses because the extra crews may be fully qualified and simply unable to work the same night. A cap on simultaneous crews is not a finding about competence."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Conditional reversal/negation","d":3,"s":"If the lacquer is stirred during the hour it stands, it will not skin. This batch skinned.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["The lacquer skinned because it was left unstirred.","The lacquer is never stirred.","The lacquer was not stirred during the hour it stood.","Every skinned batch in the city comes from this shop.","Stirring lacquer costs more than leaving it."],"a":2,"e":"Stirring is sufficient to prevent a skin. This batch skinned, so that sufficient condition was absent. The attractive choice — The lacquer skinned because it was left unstirred. — loses because \"because\" names the missing stir as the cause. The conditional only says stirring would have prevented the skin. Something else could have skinned a batch that was also unstirred."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Quantifier scope","d":3,"s":"Most of the referees who worked a final this year have also worked a semifinal. Several referees who worked a final this year have never worked a semifinal.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["Most referees who worked a semifinal this year also worked a final.","Every referee who worked a final this year worked a semifinal.","No referee worked both a final and a semifinal this year.","At least one referee who worked a final this year also worked a semifinal.","Several referees who worked only semifinals have never worked a final."],"a":3,"e":"\"Most\" of a group includes at least one. So at least one final referee has also worked a semifinal. The second sentence leaves room for several exceptions. The attractive choice — Most referees who worked a semifinal this year also worked a final. — loses because the overlap is stated from the final side. It does not say what share of the semifinal referees came from finals. Reversing \"most\" does not follow."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Conditional chain","d":2,"s":"If a crate is stamped for export, it is weighed. If it is weighed, a moisture card is attached. Crate K has been stamped for export. Crate L has not been weighed.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["A moisture card has been attached to crate L.","Crate K has not been weighed.","Crate L was stamped for export.","Every crate with a moisture card attached has been stamped for export.","A moisture card has been attached to crate K."],"a":4,"e":"An export stamp brings a weighing, and a weighing brings a moisture card. Crate K was stamped, so both steps apply to it. The attractive choice — A moisture card has been attached to crate L. — loses because crate L was not weighed, so the second conditional never starts for it. Nothing attaches a moisture card to crate L. The letter is easy to swap with K."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":2,"s":"The hatchery shipped the same number of crates in both weeks. Crates of day-old chicks were a smaller share of the crates in the second week than in the first.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["Fewer crates of day-old chicks were shipped in the second week than in the first.","Fewer birds of every age left the hatchery in the second week than in the first.","Day-old chicks cost less to ship than older birds.","No crate of chicks was shipped in the first week.","The second week included a holiday."],"a":0,"e":"Equal crate totals and a smaller chick share fix a smaller chick-crate count in the second week. The attractive choice — Fewer birds of every age left the hatchery in the second week than in the first. — loses because the statements set the crate totals equal, so a smaller chick share does not shrink the whole shipment. Other ages can take a larger share."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":2,"s":"In four separate summers, courtyards that planted shade trees had fewer afternoon ambulance calls than those same courtyards had the summer before, and the logs record no change in the hours the courtyards were open or in the age mix of the people using them.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The shade trees were the sole cause of every ambulance call those courtyards avoided.","The shade trees are a credible candidate for having contributed to the drop in afternoon ambulance calls in those courtyards.","Courtyards without shade trees had more ambulance calls than courtyards with trees in every city that keeps such logs.","Shade trees will be planted in every courtyard in the district next summer.","People who sit in a shaded courtyard never need an ambulance."],"a":1,"e":"The same courtyards improved after the trees went in, four times, with the obvious operational changes absent from the logs. That supports a cautious candidate. It does not pin every avoided call on the trees alone. The attractive choice — The shade trees were the sole cause of every ambulance call those courtyards avoided. — loses because \"sole cause\" and \"every\" call close doors the logs never close. Weather, for example, can still differ. The strong wording outruns a pattern that only makes the trees plausible."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":3,"s":"The organ-blower log is the only place a failed start is recorded. Every failed start this season would have been recorded in it. The tuner has the complete log, and it names no failed start.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The organ blower will have no failed start next season.","The tuner personally plays every service.","The organ blower had no failed start this season.","The blower is the only machine in the loft.","A failed start is more expensive than a clean one."],"a":2,"e":"A failed start would have been entered, the log is complete, and the log names none. The supported conclusion is that none failed this season. The attractive choice — The organ blower will have no failed start next season. — loses because the log speaks to this season. Next season is a new period, and nothing says the machine or the practice will stay as it is."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":2,"s":"The only people asked about dropping the Thursday matinee were season-ticket holders who already attend it, and most of them asked that it be kept. People who do not hold season tickets were not asked.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["Most of the theater's patrons want the Thursday matinee dropped.","The matinee should be cancelled.","Every season-ticket holder has seen the current play.","The replies do not establish that most of the theater's patrons want the Thursday matinee kept.","Thursday is the only day the balcony is open."],"a":3,"e":"The sample is people who already come. Their wish to keep the matinee does not establish the patrons' majority. That negative conclusion is what the narrow sample supports. The attractive choice — Most of the theater's patrons want the Thursday matinee dropped. — loses because silence from nonholders does not show that they want the matinee dropped. They might favor it, split, or have no view. Opposition is the flashy reverse, and it is not supported."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":2,"s":"A clinic booked fewer appointments this April than last April. Vaccination appointments were a larger share of the appointments this April than last April.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The clinic booked fewer vaccination appointments this April than last April.","Vaccinations take longer than routine checkups.","The clinic will book fewer appointments next April.","No checkup was booked this April.","The figures do not establish that the clinic booked more vaccination appointments this April than last April."],"a":4,"e":"A larger share of a smaller total can be more vaccination appointments, fewer, or the same number. The two facts do not establish an increase. The attractive choice — The clinic booked fewer vaccination appointments this April than last April. — loses because a smaller total does not drag every subcategory down when its share rises. \"Fewer vaccination appointments\" is one possibility, not the one the figures pick out."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Weak analogy","d":3,"s":"A net stopped bird losses on a cherry row, where birds were taking fruit from open branches. The pear row loses fruit to wasps, and there is nowhere on that row to hang a net.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The cherry result is a weak reason to expect a net to cut losses on the pear row.","A net would increase the pear row's losses to wasps.","The pear row should be abandoned.","Every cherry is heavier than every pear.","Wasps also take fruit on the cherry row."],"a":0,"e":"The mechanism the net fixed is not the pear row's mechanism, and the pear row has nowhere to hang a net. The transfer is poorly supported. That is as far as the facts go. The attractive choice — A net would increase the pear row's losses to wasps. — loses because a weak analogy fails to support a benefit. It does not support the opposite prediction that losses would rise."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":2,"s":"A donor's letter is the only push to discard the local-history shelves, and the letter cites no circulation figures. Discarding a shelf is governed by the library's retention rule. The letter never applies that rule.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The retention rule requires the library to keep the local-history shelves.","The donor's letter does not establish that the shelves should be discarded.","The donor has used the local-history shelves.","Letters are a poor way to memorize retention rules.","The library has discarded other shelves this year."],"a":1,"e":"The only push is a letter with no circulation figures, and the governing rule is one the letter never applies. That supports the negative claim that the letter does not establish the discard. The attractive choice — The retention rule requires the library to keep the local-history shelves. — loses because the rule is named, and its content is not given. It might require the discard once circulation is checked. \"Keep the shelves\" adds a verdict the statements do not contain."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":3,"s":"The observatory's dome motor has opened on the first try in each of twenty nightly checks, always on the same circuit and with the same operator. Tonight's check is scheduled on that circuit with that operator, and the motor has not been altered since the last check.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The dome motor cannot fail to open in tonight's check.","The dome was built twenty years ago.","There is good reason to expect the dome motor to open on the first try in tonight's check.","The observatory will not open the dome tonight.","The operator is the only person allowed to buy the circuit breaker."],"a":2,"e":"Twenty matched successes and an unaltered motor, with tonight's conditions held to the same circuit and operator, support a reasonable expectation. They do not make a failure impossible. The attractive choice — The dome motor cannot fail to open in tonight's check. — loses because \"cannot fail\" converts a strong pattern into a guarantee. A matched record raises the probability. It does not close every mechanical surprise."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"False dichotomy","d":2,"s":"The guild will not cancel the riverside firing. It will delay the firing by one hour if the wind is above the posted limit at the advertised time, and that delay has kept every previous firing intact. Wind is the only threat on tonight's forecast.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["Tonight's firing will begin at the advertised time.","The guild has never fired indoors.","Wind is forecast for the entire weekend.","The forecast does not show that tonight's firing will be lost.","Visitors prefer a delayed firing to a cancelled one."],"a":3,"e":"Cancellation is off the table, and the only forecast threat is wind the guild already rides out by waiting an hour. The facts support that the firing is not shown to be lost. The attractive choice — Tonight's firing will begin at the advertised time. — loses because if the wind is above the limit at the advertised time, the guild delays. The firing may start an hour late. \"At the advertised time\" ignores the delay the stimulus describes."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":2,"s":"The irritation assay would have detected the rash compound in any batch that contained it. Every batch in this lot was assayed, and the compound was not detected.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The dye is safe for every use a customer might invent.","The assay is the cheapest test the lab runs.","The lot was mixed on a Tuesday.","Customers prefer unscented dye.","No batch in this lot contained the rash compound the assay detects."],"a":4,"e":"An assay that would have found the compound, run on every batch, and negative, supports the absence of that compound in this lot. The attractive choice — The dye is safe for every use a customer might invent. — loses because safety for every possible use is far wider than one compound in one lot. The assay does not speak to uses, doses, or compounds it does not detect."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":3,"s":"A gallery sold the same number of works in both seasons. Drawings were a smaller share of the works in the later season.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The gallery sold fewer drawings in the later season than in the earlier season.","The gallery sold fewer works of every kind in the later season.","Drawings take longer to frame than paintings.","The gallery will sell fewer drawings next season.","No painting was sold in the earlier season."],"a":0,"e":"An unchanged total and a smaller drawing share fix a smaller drawing count. That is the supported comparison. The attractive choice — The gallery sold fewer works of every kind in the later season. — loses because \"every kind\" does not follow. With the total fixed, some other kind of work took a larger share."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":2,"s":"Only riders already on the night bus were asked, and most of them want the route kept. People already riding are not a stand-in for the neighborhood. So the transit board has not shown that most residents want the night bus kept.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["People already riding the night bus are not a stand-in for the neighborhood.","The transit board has not shown that most residents want the night bus kept.","Most riders already on the night bus want the route kept.","The transit board should cancel the night bus.","Residents who never ride were asked a different question."],"a":1,"e":"The narrow sample and the warning about stand-ins support a verdict about what the board has shown. That verdict is the conclusion. The attractive choice — People already riding the night bus are not a stand-in for the neighborhood. — loses because the stand-in sentence is the rule the argument uses. It would still be doing its work if the verdict were left unstated. It is support, and it is the sentence a quick read is most likely to underline."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":3,"s":"Storing a volume flat is sufficient to prevent a cracked spine and is not the only way. This volume's spine cracked, and an archivist concludes that it therefore was not stored flat. That conclusion does follow from the conditional, but the archivist also concludes that the upright storage caused the crack. The causal half does not follow.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["Storing a volume flat is sufficient to prevent a cracked spine.","This volume was stored flat.","The archivist's further claim that upright storage caused the crack does not follow.","The archivist should be reassigned.","Every cracked spine was stored upright."],"a":2,"e":"The conditional is cited so that the causal extra can be separated from the valid half. The argument's point is that the causal half does not follow. The attractive choice — Storing a volume flat is sufficient to prevent a cracked spine. — loses because the flat-storage rule is the premise. The argument does not stop at repeating it. It uses the rule to reject the archivist's causal addition."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":2,"s":"A tour guide's blog is the only voice for relocating the trailhead, and the blog cites no survey. The board must apply the trail standard. A blog that never applies that standard is not a reason to relocate the trailhead. The blog therefore does not give the board a reason to relocate it.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["The board must apply the trail standard.","The guide surveyed the trail before posting.","The trail standard requires the trailhead to stay.","The blog does not give the board a reason to relocate the trailhead.","The board should ignore every public comment about the trail."],"a":3,"e":"The standard and the missing survey support a judgment about the blog. The judgment is that the blog is not a reason to relocate the trailhead. The attractive choice — The board must apply the trail standard. — loses because the duty to apply the standard is a premise. The argument uses the rule to set the blog aside. It does not stop at reminding the board of the rule."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":3,"s":"The harbor chart is called accurate because it matches the pilot's sketch, and the sketch is called accurate because it matches the chart. A clerk concludes that the chart is accurate. Each document is given no support except the other.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["The harbor chart matches the pilot's sketch.","The pilot drew the sketch before the chart was printed.","The clerk should order a new sounding.","Every chart that matches a sketch is accurate.","The clerk's conclusion that the chart is accurate gives each document no support except the other."],"a":4,"e":"The mutual match is described so that the clerk's conclusion can be placed on it. The argument's point is that the conclusion has no support outside that circle. The attractive choice — The harbor chart matches the pilot's sketch. — loses because the match itself is the fact the argument cites. It is not the claim being made about the clerk's reasoning, which is where the argument comes to rest."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":2,"s":"Vinyl's share of a record shop's sales fell, and the shop sold the same number of records in both months. From the share alone, a clerk concludes that every format declined. The clerk's conclusion ignores the fixed total.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["The clerk's conclusion that every format declined ignores the fixed total.","Vinyl's share of the shop's sales fell while the number of records stayed the same.","The shop sold fewer records in the later month.","The clerk should recount the vinyl by hand.","Cassettes are the only format the shop sells."],"a":0,"e":"The share and the total are the facts. The argument uses them to criticize the clerk. That criticism is the main conclusion. The attractive choice — Vinyl's share of the shop's sales fell while the number of records stayed the same. — loses because the share-and-total sentence is the evidence. It can be true and still not be the claim the argument is making about the clerk."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":2,"s":"Birds, not hail, take the orchard fruit, and there is no frame on which to hang a screen. The greenhouse screen stopped a different kind of loss. So that result is a poor reason to expect the same cut in the orchard.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["Birds, not hail, take the orchard fruit.","The greenhouse result is a poor reason to expect a screen to cut losses in the orchard.","The orchard should be screened this week.","Hail takes the orchard fruit as well.","A screen would raise the orchard's losses."],"a":1,"e":"The birds and the missing frame explain why the greenhouse result does not travel. The conclusion is the verdict on that result as a reason. The attractive choice — Birds, not hail, take the orchard fruit. — loses because the bird sentence is a premise about how the orchard loses fruit. The argument cites it on the way to a conclusion about the analogy."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":2,"s":"Either the orchard is netted before dawn or the first picking loses the outer rows. The orchard will not be netted before dawn. Therefore, the first picking will lose the outer rows.","q":"The argument proceeds by","c":["showing that no third course could save the unnetted outer rows","citing the picker's count of rows finished per hour","reaching a result by ruling out one of two alternatives that the argument treats as exhaustive","inferring a cause from the fact that late netting and lost rows have occurred together","generalizing from one picking to every picking on the calendar"],"a":2,"e":"The argument states an either-or, rejects the dawn netting, and takes the remaining side. It does not demonstrate that the list was complete. The attractive choice — showing that no third course could save the unnetted outer rows — loses because completeness is assumed in the either-or, not shown. The attractive description credits the argument with a proof it never attempts."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":3,"s":"The kiln wall cracked, and the firing was left unwatched. The shop lead concludes that leaving it unwatched caused the crack, since the crack was noticed after the watch was skipped.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately describes the method of reasoning?","c":["applying a general rule that an unwatched firing always cracks a kiln wall","rejecting a causal claim because of the lead's financial interest in the kiln","inferring that a property of the crack is a property of the shop","treating the order of two events as sufficient to show that the earlier event caused the later one","concluding that the watch was skipped because the wall cracked"],"a":3,"e":"The only support named is that the crack was noticed after the watch was skipped. The lead reads that order as causation. No general \"always\" rule is stated. The attractive choice — applying a general rule that an unwatched firing always cracks a kiln wall — loses because a universal rule about unwatched firings is not in the argument. The lead relies on sequence in this firing. The attractive description imports a principle the lead did not use."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":2,"s":"Most commuters who already ride the dawn ferry want the sailing kept. The clerk concludes that most harbor workers want the sailing kept.","q":"The argument proceeds by","c":["showing that harbor workers who skip the dawn ferry were surveyed and disagreed","defining \"harbor worker\" one way in the premise and another way in the conclusion","concluding that the sailing caused ridership because the two rose together","rejecting the riders' view because of the clerk's schedule","taking the majority view of people who already ride to be the majority view of the harbor workers generally"],"a":4,"e":"The premise is a majority among riders. The conclusion is a majority of harbor workers. The method is that transfer, with no further sample described. The attractive choice — showing that harbor workers who skip the dawn ferry were surveyed and disagreed — loses because harbor workers who skip the dawn ferry are not said to have been surveyed. The attractive description adds a conflicting poll the clerk does not use."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":3,"s":"Program notes were 40 percent of a concert booklet's pages and are now 9 percent. The editor concludes that the booklet now contains fewer pages of program notes, without comparing the number of pages in the two booklets.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately describes the method of reasoning?","c":["inferring a change in a count from a change in a percentage, with no information about the relevant totals","establishing that the booklet's total number of pages fell between the two concerts","appealing to an editor's authority about which pages are program notes","treating a feature of each note as a feature of the whole booklet","rejecting the percentage because the editor dislikes program notes"],"a":0,"e":"The only figure given is the program-note share. The editor reads it as a count. The totals are explicitly unused. The attractive choice — establishing that the booklet's total number of pages fell between the two concerts — loses because the totals are exactly what the argument leaves out. The attractive description says the argument established a fact it never had."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":2,"s":"A cartographer who cites no sounding says the channel buoy should be moved. A harbormaster treats that sentence as settling the question.","q":"The harbormaster's argument proceeds by","c":["showing that the cartographer took soundings and applied the harbor's channel standard","treating a cartographer's unsupported assertion as decisive on a question the assertion does not address with depth evidence","inferring that the buoy drifted because a move and a drift were discussed together","generalizing from one buoy to every buoy in the harbor","dismissing the cartographer because of a personal disagreement"],"a":1,"e":"The harbormaster's entire move is to adopt the sentence. No sounding is supplied. The method is reliance on that bare assertion. The attractive choice — showing that the cartographer took soundings and applied the harbor's channel standard — loses because a sounding under the channel standard is what the argument lacks. The attractive description awards the harbormaster a method the stimulus withholds."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":2,"s":"Each pamphlet in the carton is light enough for one stamp. The clerk concludes that the carton is light enough for one stamp.","q":"The argument proceeds by","c":["showing that the carton weighs no more than the heaviest pamphlet in it","appealing to the clerk's authority about postage","attributing to a carton a limit that has been established only for each pamphlet in the carton","inferring a cause from the order in which the pamphlets were packed","using \"stamp\" in the premise for a legal approval and in the conclusion for postage"],"a":2,"e":"The premise is about each pamphlet. The conclusion is about the carton. Nothing is said that would cap the carton at the heaviest pamphlet. The attractive choice — showing that the carton weighs no more than the heaviest pamphlet in it — loses because a cap at the heaviest pamphlet would have made the conclusion safe, and the argument does not provide it. The attractive description states a missing premise as if it were the method already used."},
{"t":"Role","pattern":"Statement role","d":2,"s":"**Replies from stallholders do not represent the night market's customers.** Only stallholders who already set up on Fridays were asked, and most of them want the Friday hours kept. So the market has not shown that most customers want those hours kept.","q":"The boldfaced statement plays which one of the following roles in the argument?","c":["It is the main conclusion of the argument.","It is an intermediate conclusion used to support a recommendation to cancel Friday hours.","It is a hypothesis the argument rejects.","It is a premise offered in support of the conclusion that the market has not shown what most customers want.","It is background about stall rents that the reasoning does not rely on."],"a":3,"e":"The final sentence is the conclusion, and it is about what the market has shown. The boldfaced sentence gives the sampling rule that supports that conclusion. The attractive choice — It is the main conclusion of the argument. — loses because the boldfaced sentence is general and prominent, so it looks like the point. The argument uses it to reach a further verdict about this poll."},
{"t":"Role","pattern":"Statement role","d":3,"s":"The pressure gauge would have recorded any drop below the line, and it recorded none, so the line held. **The weld was therefore performed by a certified fitter.**","q":"The boldfaced statement plays which one of the following roles in the argument?","c":["It is a premise reporting what the pressure gauge recorded.","It is an intermediate calculation of how far the pressure fell.","It is a hypothesis the argument rejects.","It is background about certification fees that the reasoning does not use.","It is the conclusion drawn from the claim that the line held."],"a":4,"e":"The gauge and the holding of the line are offered in support of the boldfaced claim about the fitter. Nothing further is inferred from that claim. The attractive choice — It is a premise reporting what the pressure gauge recorded. — loses because the gauge reading is the earlier clause, not the boldfaced sentence. The attractive role assigns the premise's content to the conclusion about who welded."},
{"t":"Role","pattern":"Statement role","d":2,"s":"**The mural will not be simplified.** The curator says that either the mural is simplified or it misses the wall date, and concludes that the mural will miss the wall date.","q":"The boldfaced statement plays which one of the following roles in the argument?","c":["It is a premise that, together with the either-or claim, is offered as yielding the conclusion.","It is the conclusion that the mural will miss the wall date.","It is an alternative the curator rejects as impossible for every mural.","It is evidence that the wall date has already been missed.","It is a recommendation the argument refuses to consider."],"a":0,"e":"The conclusion is that the mural will miss the wall date. The boldfaced sentence denies the other alternative. It is one of the two premises. The attractive choice — It is the conclusion that the mural will miss the wall date. — loses because the missed wall date is what the curator is trying to establish. The boldfaced sentence states a decision already taken. The attractive role assigns the conclusion's content to the wrong clause."},
{"t":"Role","pattern":"Statement role","d":2,"s":"The editor notes that liner notes fell from 25 percent of a label's inserts to 6 percent. **The label printed fewer liner notes.** No total for either season's inserts is given.","q":"The boldfaced statement plays which one of the following roles in the argument?","c":["It is a premise stating the label's total number of inserts in the later season.","It is the conclusion the editor draws from the change in share.","It is an objection a critic raises against the editor.","It is a definition of \"liner note.\"","It is an intermediate figure from which the share is calculated."],"a":1,"e":"The share change is the noted fact. The boldfaced sentence is what the editor concludes from it. The last sentence points out that no total was supplied. The attractive choice — It is a premise stating the label's total number of inserts in the later season. — loses because a total is precisely what the argument says is missing. The boldfaced sentence states a count of liner notes, not a total of all inserts, and it is inferred rather than given."}
]);
